          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::aa01a377236c40b93227d94e",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "key_findings"
          ],
          "value": {
            "conditions": "Human advanced NSCLC or urothelial carcinomas treated with ICIs",
            "finding": "Increased relative abundances of Akkermansia muciniphila and Enterococcus hirae associated with clinical response."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::07a943c867a88980d90a169c",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "key_findings"
          ],
          "value": {
            "conditions": "Human solid tumors treated with anti-PD-1",
            "finding": "Higher fecal short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), particularly propionate, associated with longer progression-free survival in some cohorts, though conflicting results exist for butyrate and other SCFAs across different cohorts."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::fa42d4d99ed38acb34c1d37b",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "key_findings"
          ],
          "value": {
            "conditions": "Patients receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics prior to or during ICI therapy",
            "finding": "Antibiotic use is consistently associated with reduced ICI efficacy, shorter overall survival, and shorter progression-free survival."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::dedbea0d3515dd54bf16aa5d",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "key_findings"
          ],
          "value": {
            "conditions": "ICI-associated colitis pathogenesis",
            "finding": "Specific bacteria (e.g., Bacteroides fragilis, Burkholderia cepacia) may protect against colitis by promoting regulatory T cells; conversely, increases in Firmicutes have been linked to higher incidence of colitis."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::c78e7cc967833bf2f392d7c6",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "key_findings"
          ],
          "value": {
            "conditions": "Mechanistic pathways",
            "finding": "Two major mechanisms identified: (1) Antigen-independent: Microbes modulate innate/adaptive immunity (e.g., dendritic cell activation, CD8+ T cell infiltration); (2) Antigen-specific: Molecular mimicry where microbial epitopes cross-react with tumor neoantigens."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::c29dfd46f6d921851086de8c",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "paper_kind"
          ],
          "value": "review",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::9d79e2be028afafd60ebc2af",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "problem_or_question"
          ],
          "value": "How does the intestinal microbiome modulate the efficacy and toxicity of ICIs? What are the specific microbial signatures and mechanisms involved, and how can these insights be translated into clinical biomarkers or therapies?",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::d968e3d265dd2454d8368edd",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "research_scope"
          ],
          "value": "The review focuses on the role of the intestinal microbiome and microbial-derived metabolites in modulating responses to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in cancer patients, specifically addressing efficacy outcomes (tumor response, survival) and toxicities (immune-related adverse events like colitis). It covers preclinical mouse models and retrospective/prospective human clinical studies.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::c9ca196e0f93ee8c629cc194",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "work_summary"
          ],
          "value": "This paper reviews the emerging evidence linking the gut microbiome to the efficacy and toxicity of immune checkpoint blockade immunotherapy. It synthesizes findings from preclinical models and clinical studies to identify specific bacterial taxa and metabolites associated with ICI response or resistance. The authors discuss two main mechanistic categories: antigen-independent modulation of innate and adaptive immunity, and antigen-specific cross-reactivity between microbial and tumor antigens. Additionally, the review examines the negative impact of antibiotic use on ICI efficacy and the potential of fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) to restore responsiveness or treat colitis. It highlights inconsistencies across studies regarding specific beneficial bacteria and calls for larger prospective interventional trials to establish microbiome-based biomarkers and therapeutic strategies.",
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::15f73eb5eadb303dd3b9f876",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions"
          ],
          "value": {
            "boundaries": "Associations are inconsistent across studies; many findings are from retrospective or small prospective cohorts. Causal direction is often inferred rather than proven. Specific taxa may vary by tumor type and geography.",
            "contribution": "Provides a catalog of specific bacterial taxa (e.g., Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium, Faecalibacterium) and metabolites (propionate, inosine) associated with improved ICI response in various solid tumors, along with taxa linked to poor response (Bacteroides, Ruminococcus obeum). Also details the negative impact of broad-spectrum antibiotics on ICI outcomes.",
            "facet_id": "F1"
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::b52a2a2c18a4fca342b2c728",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions"
          ],
          "value": {
            "boundaries": "Mechanistic evidence is largely derived from preclinical models or small-scale human studies. The prevalence and clinical significance of antigen mimicry are still being elucidated. Inconsistencies in human data limit definitive mechanistic claims.",
            "contribution": "Outlines two distinct mechanistic pathways: (1) Antigen-independent modulation via innate/adaptive immune cell engagement (e.g., DC activation, CD8+ T cell infiltration); (2) Antigen-specific cross-reactivity between microbial and tumor neoantigens. Discusses how specific bacteria might promote regulatory T cells to prevent colitis.",
            "facet_id": "F2"
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::825500cc93825fbbc3024cf3",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "broader_review_uses"
          ],
          "value": {
            "material": "Introduction to ICIs, their mechanism (blocking inhibitory pathways), and the problem of heterogeneous response and immune-related adverse events (colitis)."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
          "snapshot_ids": [
            "snapshot::16691695321f5acfe09a283c"
          ],
          "author_chapter_id": "Ch1",
          "author_messages_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\guided_body_compact_auto_metadata_20261009_12cny\\LIVE\\stages\\author_001\\47be7e22b5ff720e1d0f995795f678f363ed33890ab836f8c1baae006ee530dc\\attempt_001\\MESSAGES.json",
          "raw_source_handle": "P0291"
        },
        {
          "atom_id": "atom::9ae21607fe4cc933f2b679c3",
          "source_handle": "P0291",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "broader_review_uses"
          ],
          "value": {
            "material": "Acknowledgment of sequencing center heterogeneity, geographical/population differences, and the need for large-scale prospective studies similar to GWAS."
          },
          "role": "evidence",
